<%- include('partials/header') %>

<div class="container">
    <div class="content-box hero">
        <h1>About Circles</h1>
        <p>The fundamental shape that has captivated mathematicians, artists, and philosophers for millennia.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-box">
        <h2>The Definition of a Circle</h2>
        <p>A circle is defined as the locus of all points in a plane that are at a fixed distance (called the radius) from a fixed point (called the center). This simple definition leads to one of the most elegant and important shapes in all of geometry.</p>
        
        <div class="circle-display" style="margin: 2rem 0;">
            <div class="circle" style="width: 200px; height: 200px; background: linear-gradient(135deg, #667eea 0%, #764ba2 100%); position: relative;">
                <div style="position: absolute; width: 4px; height: 100px; background: #ffd700; top: 50%; left: 50%; transform: translate(-50%, -50%) rotate(45deg); transform-origin: bottom;"></div>
                <span style="z-index: 1;">r</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="content-box">
        <h2>Historical Significance</h2>
        <p>Circles have played a crucial role in human civilization since ancient times:</p>
        
        <div class="info-grid">
            <div class="info-card">
                <h3>Ancient Egypt</h3>
                <p>The Egyptians used circles extensively in construction, including the circular measurements for the pyramids. They approximated π as 3.16, remarkably close to the actual value.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="info-card">
                <h3>Ancient Greece</h3>
                <p>Greek mathematicians like Euclid and Archimedes made groundbreaking discoveries about circles. Archimedes calculated π to remarkable precision and explored the properties of circular areas.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="info-card">
                <h3>Renaissance Era</h3>
                <p>Artists during the Renaissance considered the circle a symbol of divine perfection. Leonardo da Vinci's "Vitruvian Man" demonstrates the circle's relationship to ideal proportions.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="info-card">
                <h3>Modern Mathematics</h3>
                <p>Today, circles are fundamental to trigonometry, calculus, and complex analysis. They appear in physics, engineering, and computer graphics.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="content-box">
        <h2>Parts of a Circle</h2>
        <ul style="line-height: 2.5; font-size: 1.1rem;">
            <li><strong>Center:</strong> The fixed point that is equidistant from all points on the circle</li>
            <li><strong>Radius:</strong> The distance from the center to any point on the circle</li>
            <li><strong>Diameter:</strong> A line segment passing through the center with endpoints on the circle (twice the radius)</li>
            <li><strong>Circumference:</strong> The perimeter or boundary of the circle</li>
            <li><strong>Chord:</strong> A line segment connecting any two points on the circle</li>
            <li><strong>Arc:</strong> A portion of the circumference</li>
            <li><strong>Sector:</strong> A region bounded by two radii and an arc</li>
            <li><strong>Tangent:</strong> A line that touches the circle at exactly one point</li>
            <li><strong>Secant:</strong> A line that intersects the circle at two points</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="content-box">
        <h2>Circles in Nature</h2>
        <p>Circular patterns are abundant in the natural world, demonstrating nature's preference for this efficient shape:</p>
        <div class="info-grid">
            <div class="info-card">
                <h3>🌕 Celestial Bodies</h3>
                <p>Planets, stars, and moons form spheres due to gravity pulling matter equally in all directions, appearing as circles from our perspective.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="info-card">
                <h3>🌊 Ripples</h3>
                <p>When a drop falls into water, the disturbance spreads outward in perfect circles, following the principles of wave propagation.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="info-card">
                <h3>🌳 Tree Rings</h3>
                <p>Trees grow in concentric circles, adding a new ring each year. These circles tell the story of the tree's life and environmental conditions.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="info-card">
                <h3>🌸 Flowers</h3>
                <p>Many flowers arrange their petals in circular patterns, optimizing sunlight capture and pollinator attraction.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="info-card">
                <h3>👁️ Eyes</h3>
                <p>The iris and pupil of eyes are circular, allowing for optimal light gathering and focus adjustment.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="info-card">
                <h3>🕸️ Spider Webs</h3>
                <p>Orb-weaver spiders create circular webs that efficiently catch prey while using minimal material.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="content-box">
        <h2>Symbolic Meanings</h2>
        <p>Across cultures and throughout history, the circle has held deep symbolic significance:</p>
        <ul style="line-height: 2; font-size: 1.1rem; column-count: 2; column-gap: 2rem;">
            <li>Eternity and infinity (no beginning or end)</li>
            <li>Unity and wholeness</li>
            <li>The cycle of life and seasons</li>
            <li>Divine perfection</li>
            <li>The sun and moon</li>
            <li>Protection (circle of protection)</li>
            <li>Community (sitting in a circle)</li>
            <li>Completeness and fulfillment</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

